<capabilities>
<p>The prototype Behemoth mounts a minimal defensive armament consisting entirely of energy weapons, reflecting its civilian cargo role. Each side of the hull carries two ER large lasers and four medium lasers, while the aft arc mounts two additional medium lasers. Two hundred and ninety-seven single heat sinks manage the thermal load. The vessel carries no nose-mounted weapons, relying on its flanking batteries for defense against threats approaching from any quarter.</p><p>The bulk of the vessel comprises twenty cargo decks, each capable of accommodating nearly four thousand tons of cargo, for a total capacity exceeding seventy-five thousand tons. A central support spoke with lifts provides access to each deck. Twenty small craft bays facilitate cargo transfer operations to and from planetary surfaces. The massive drive system occupies several lower decks and can provide approximately 1.5 Gs of acceleration, though sustained operation at full power causes stress cracks in numerous components. Most captains rarely operate the drive above half capacity to avoid expensive repairs.</p>
</capabilities>

<overview>
<p>The Behemoth is the largest DropShip class ever constructed, a hundred-thousand-ton Spheroid cargo hauler that dwarfs even the Mammoth class at nearly twice its tonnage. Incapable of atmospheric operations or planetary landings due to its sheer mass, the Behemoth relies on orbital cargo stations or shuttle craft to transfer goods to a planet's surface. This additional stage in loading operations increases operating costs considerably, limiting the vessel's economic appeal to most merchant lines.</p><p>Despite these limitations, the Behemoth remains indispensable for bulk cargo operations where no other vessel can match its capacity. Its twenty cargo decks can accommodate nearly eighty thousand tons of goods, making a single Behemoth transit worth dozens of trips by smaller vessels. The design has endured across centuries of service, a reflection of the soundness of its original engineering.</p>
</overview>

<deployment>
<p>The prototype Behemoth entered limited service around 2658 during the design's troubled early development phase. Only a handful of these early vessels were produced before the finalized production model superseded them, and surviving prototypes were gradually retired or upgraded to the production standard over the following decades.</p>
</deployment>

<history>
<p>The Behemoth class has a complicated design history stretching back to the mid-twenty-seventh century. The Terran Hegemony originally commissioned the design alongside the smaller Mammoth class, promising the designer that both classes could be sold to foreign powers. When the prototypes entered their final development phase around 2650, the Hegemony reneged on that promise, restricting both designs to domestic use only. In 2650, two prototypes were stolen from their orbital moorings and sold to the highest bidder, raising fears that the designs could be reverse-engineered by rival states.</p><p>The theft forced the Hegemony to reconsider its stance, and it ultimately permitted the original manufacturer to sell the design abroad. Although suspicion lingered that the manufacturer had somehow been involved in the heist, nothing was ever proven. This decision to share the design with foreign powers ensured that the Behemoth would remain in production even after the original manufacturer's demise. The finalized production variant entered service in 2782, and multiple shipyards across the Inner Sphere took up manufacturing, ensuring the class survived the Succession Wars intact.</p><p>Visual evidence confirms that at least some Clan forces have operated variants of the Behemoth, though whether these vessels were built by the Clans or captured during the Clan Invasion remains uncertain. Clan-operated examples have reportedly featured upgraded energy weapon suites, though detailed configuration data remains scarce.</p>
</history>

